fied\",\"summary\":\"An ed25519 CA keypair was generated at /etc/ssh/ca/ssh_ca with restrictive private-key permissions (600) and a readable public key (644), fingerprint SHA256:UtvoUCfbeeoEV4jqF5WBxEMKLx6gjR7RW1qcYH3gBYM.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d03c313e570249a6\"]},{\"id\":\"host-cert-signed\",\"requirement\":\"Host presents a certificate signed by the CA\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The node's existing ed25519 host public key was signed by the CA into /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key-cert.pub with principals node1, 10.100.247.10, localhost, 10-year validity, and the certificate's Signing CA fingerprint matches the CA generated in the prior step.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-95abffd4b1da4cd0\"]},{\"id\":\"sshd-serves-cert\",\"requirement\":\"sshd is configured to actually offer the certificate to clients\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A HostCertificate drop-in was added and validated (sshd -t, sshd -T showing hostcertificate directive). An initial SELinux mislabel (tmp_t) on the cert file silently prevented sshd from loading it (confirmed via journalctl 'Could not load host certificate'); this was diagnosed and fixed with chcon/semanage fcontext to sshd_key_t, after which a clean sshd restart showed no load errors and a live client handshake showed the server presenting the ssh-ed25519-cert-v01 certificate.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a0a65411c5d347d0\",\"cmd-9289365148d54679\",\"cmd-9b14c2f830c141f9\",\"cmd-5ea1bb1109e6423d\",\"cmd-c293900f6dc84c36\",\"cmd-5713d67761f24d77\",\"cmd-c4dc70e7829d4a81\"]},{\"id\":\"ca-trust-no-pinning\",\"requirement\":\"A client trusting only the CA (no pinned host key) connects successfully; trust flows from the CA, not from an incidentally pinned key\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A test client with known_hosts containing only '@cert-authority * <ca pubkey>' connected successfully and the client explicitly logged 'Host is known and matches the ED25519-CERT host certificate' before authenticating and running a command. A control run with an empty known_hosts file failed with 'No matching CA found' / 'Host key verification failed', confirming the success case depended on CA trust rather than any pinned key.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c4dc70e7829d4a81\",\"cmd-7a11a2ec65434046\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All state-changing executor commands map clearly to the task: CA creation, host-cert signing, sshd config drop-in, the SELinux relabel/fcontext fix (a necessary correction for a real defect the executor caused/discovered), and sshd restarts to load the fixed config. Temporary test artifacts (test client keys, temporary known_hosts files, authorized_keys entries used only to validate host-side trust) were added and then removed in the same session (cmd-4982dca4678d4ea8, cmd-a9566b6077944161) and again after the post-reboot re-verification (cmd-373a79bf916b4faa). The mandatory node-restart protocol (systemctl reboot) was executed and is itself a required action, not an unnecessary mutation. No abandoned files, no failed services, and package-health/listening-socket baselines are unchanged in substance (only PID/boot-id churn from the reboot) per the lifecycle comparison.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4982dca4678d4ea8\",\"cmd-a9566b6077944161\",\"cmd-373a79bf916b4faa\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor created an ed25519 SSH CA on node1, signed the host's ed25519 key into a certificate, configured sshd to serve it, root-caused and fixed a real SELinux mislabeling defect that was silently blocking certificate loading, and directly demonstrated (via live SSH client transcripts) that a client trusting only the CA's public key connects and validates the host via the certificate — with a matching negative control proving the trust is CA-derived. The mandatory reboot protocol was also completed with a clearly changed boot_id (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots) and re-verified post-reboot service and CA-trust state, and all test artifacts were cleaned up.\",\"limitations\":[\"Only one client trust round-trip was captured per state (pre-reboot and post-reboot); no evidence of testing a hostname-based principal beyond the IP-based check, though 'node1' and 'localhost' principals were included in the certificate.\",\"Evidence is drawn from ssh -v/-vvv debug output and journalctl from within the executor's own session rather than a fully independent third-party client, though this is standard and sufficient for this kind of verification.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"ca-created","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"An ed25519 CA keypair was generated at /etc/ssh/ca/ssh_ca with restrictive private-key permissions (600) and a readable public key (644), fingerprint SHA256:UtvoUCfbeeoEV4jqF5WBxEMKLx6gjR7RW1qcYH3gBYM.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d03c313e570249a6"]},{"id":"host-cert-signed","requirement":"Host presents a certificate signed by the CA","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node's existing ed25519 host public key was signed by the CA into /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key-cert.pub with principals node1, 10.100.247.10, localhost, 10-year validity, and the certificate's Signing CA fingerprint matches the CA generated in the prior step.","evidence_ids":["cmd-95abffd4b1da4cd0"]},{"id":"sshd-serves-cert","requirement":"sshd is configured to actually offer the certificate to clients","status":"satisfied","summary":"A HostCertificate drop-in was added and validated (sshd -t, sshd -T showing hostcertificate directive). An initial SELinux mislabel (tmp_t) on the cert file silently prevented sshd from loading it (confirmed via journalctl 'Could not load host certificate'); this was diagnosed and fixed with chcon/semanage fcontext to sshd_key_t, after which a clean sshd restart showed no load errors and a live client handshake showed the server presenting the ssh-ed25519-cert-v01 certificate.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a0a65411c5d347d0","cmd-9289365148d54679","cmd-9b14c2f830c141f9","cmd-5ea1bb1109e6423d","cmd-c293900f6dc84c36","cmd-5713d67761f24d77","cmd-c4dc70e7829d4a81"]},{"id":"ca-trust-no-pinning","requirement":"A client trusting only the CA (no pinned host key) connects successfully; trust flows from the CA, not from an incidentally pinned key","status":"satisfied","summary":"A test client with known_hosts containing only '@cert-authority * <ca pubkey>' connected successfully and the client explicitly logged 'Host is known and matches the ED25519-CERT host certificate' before authenticating and running a command. A control run with an empty known_hosts file failed with 'No matching CA found' / 'Host key verification failed', confirming the success case depended on CA trust rather than any pinned key.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c4dc70e7829d4a81","cmd-7a11a2ec65434046"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"All state-changing executor commands map clearly to the task: CA creation, host-cert signing, sshd config drop-in, the SELinux relabel/fcontext fix (a necessary correction for a real defect the executor caused/discovered), and sshd restarts to load the fixed config. Temporary test artifacts (test client keys, temporary known_hosts files, authorized_keys entries used only to validate host-side trust) were added and then removed in the same session (cmd-4982dca4678d4ea8, cmd-a9566b6077944161) and again after the post-reboot re-verification (cmd-373a79bf916b4faa). The mandatory node-restart protocol (systemctl reboot) was executed and is itself a required action, not an unnecessary mutation. No abandoned files, no failed services, and package-health/listening-socket baselines are unchanged in substance (only PID/boot-id churn from the reboot) per the lifecycle comparison.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4982dca4678d4ea8","cmd-a9566b6077944161","cmd-373a79bf916b4faa","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The executor created an ed25519 SSH CA on node1, signed the host's ed25519 key into a certificate, configured sshd to serve it, root-caused and fixed a real SELinux mislabeling defect that was silently blocking certificate loading, and directly demonstrated (via live SSH client transcripts) that a client trusting only the CA's public key connects and validates the host via the certificate — with a matching negative control proving the trust is CA-derived. The mandatory reboot protocol was also completed with a clearly changed boot_id (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots) and re-verified post-reboot service and CA-trust state, and all test artifacts were cleaned up.","limitations":["Only one client trust round-trip was captured per state (pre-reboot and post-reboot); no evidence of testing a hostname-based principal beyond the IP-based check, though 'node1' and 'localhost' principals were included in the certificate.","Evidence is drawn from ssh -v/-vvv debug output and journalctl from within the executor's own session rather than a fully independent third-party client, though this is standard and sufficient for this kind of verification."]},"ttft_ms":14973,"type":"result","duration_ms":51013,"uuid":"a0a7202c-c8a4-40ba-9a44-971ea8a7954e","ttft_stream_ms":1910,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":2832,"queued_turn_count":0}
